+---
+
+## 18. Two calls, roles first; substantiveness counted, not rated (v0.10)
+
+**Status: implemented locally and run on the 290-passage subset (2026-10-31), not on a corpus.** The
+subset results are at the end of this entry; they decide what changes before v0.10 replaces v0.9.
+
+**Before (v0.4 to v0.9).** One call per 40 passages returned the whole label. The schema enforced
+that the role-dependent fields are given if and only if their role is (`risk_types` with `risk`,
+`adoption_stage` and `business_functions` with `adoption`, `governance_responses` with
+`governance`, `mit_subdomains` with `risk` or `incident`), so a wrong role took its fields with it
+inside the same answer. `substantiveness` was one of five ordered levels (`boilerplate`,
+`generic`, `specific`, `detailed`, `quantified`), rated by the coder against cumulative criteria.
+
+**What the validation of `us-2025h1_V3` showed** (`analysis/human_validation/results/us-2025h1_V3/agreement.md`;
+290 passages, 7 raters, 59 human-human pairs): `roles` as a set has human-human kappa 0.48 and
+human-model 0.50; `risk` alone 0.79 / 0.75, `adoption` 0.37 / 0.55, `governance` 0.40 / 0.55,
+`general` below zero. `substantiveness` has human-human kappa 0.36 and human-model 0.30, the
+weakest field after the disclosure traits; on the one-against-40 comparison
+(`docs/one-vs-40-passages-per-request.md`) the same model agreed with itself on it at 0.49 only.
+
+**New.**
+
+- *Two calls* (`src/airo/taxonomy.py`: `RolesLabel`, `DetailsLabel`, `merge`; `codebook(stage)`;
+  `src/airo/coders/gemini.py`: `--stage roles|details|single`, `--roles-from`). The roles call
+  decides `ai_reference`, `frame` and `roles` with a codebook of about 11,000 characters
+  (`codebook_roles.md`). The details call gets the roles as given in each task ("do not judge
+  again, do not return") and codes every other field with `codebook_details.md` (about 45,000
+  characters, the MIT subdomains being most of it), for the passages the roles call found to be
+  about AI; a `none` passage gets its label without a request. Each answer is merged with its
+  roles label and validated as one `Label`, so the cross-field rules are unchanged and `airo
+  accept` reads the output as before. The two calls may use different models. `single` is the
+  one-call design as before, under the same taxonomy, so the two can be compared on the same
+  passages. `airo tasks` writes all three codebooks.
+- *Substantiveness as a count* (`SUBSTANTIVENESS_PARTICULARS`, `substantiveness_of`). The coder
+  ticks which of ten particulars the AI statement gives, each backed by the passage: group S, what
+  singles the statement out as this company's (`named_system`, `named_function`,
+  `named_counterparty`, `mechanism`, `figure`), and group D, what the company does or has seen
+  (`own_measure`, `responsibility`, `time_reference`, `realised_outcome`, `scope`). On acceptance
+  the pipeline derives `substantiveness_score` (the number of ticks, 0 to 10) and
+  `substantiveness` with three levels: `generic` (no tick), `company_specific` (ticks in one group
+  only), `detailed` (ticks in both). A level or score the coder gives is checked against the ticks
+  and rejected if it contradicts them; adjudication votes per particular and derives level and
+  score from the vote. Export and aggregation carry the score (mean and maximum per filing, mean per
+  report) next to the level. The level is the field the policy team's three categories map to; the
+  score is what keeps the differences the three categories hide.
+
+**Why.**
+
+- Roles gate every dependent field, and roles are where the raters disagree. In one call a
+  disagreement on roles cannot be separated from its consequences, and every refinement of the
+  roles codebook costs a run over the whole label. In two calls the roles codebook is a quarter of
+  the length, can be iterated on its own, and the first call can go to a stronger model with
+  reasoning while the second goes to a cheaper one (team decision of 2026-10-31, J. Pilcher and
+  D. Kozachek: agreement on roles is the one figure the results cannot be reported without).
+- A rating on an ordinal scale has no criterion a second coder can check, and the human-human
+  kappa of 0.36 says people do not apply the five levels the same way either. Hope, Hu and Lu
+  (2016) measure the specificity of risk-factor disclosure as a count of named entities per word
+  and validate it against student ratings on a five-point scale (correlation 0.51); the measure
+  itself is a count, not a rating. Counting defined particulars makes every disagreement a
+  disagreement about one particular, gives an ordinal score for weighted kappa and regressions,
+  and lets the three categories be defined by the kind of particulars present rather than by cut
+  points chosen after seeing the distribution. A free 1-to-10 rating by the model was considered
+  and set aside for the same reason the five levels are: nothing in it can be checked against the
+  text.
+
+**Decisions.**
+
+| Decision | Alternative considered | Reason |
+|---|---|---|
+| Roles call: `ai_reference`, `frame`, `roles`; details call: everything else | Also `ai_kinds` and the traits in the first call | They do not gate anything; keeping the first call to what gates the rest keeps its codebook short for the stronger model. |
+| Three levels from the kind of particulars (S only or D only against both) | Cut points on the score (0, 1-3, 4+) | Cut points would be set to the distribution and would move with it; the kind-based rule is fixed and mirrors the cumulative logic of v0.9 (level 4 presupposed level 3). |
+| "D only" is `company_specific`, not `generic` | `generic` (nothing singles the statement out as this company's) | "We adopted an AI acceptable-use policy and trained staff" says more than a statement any firm could make; it names what the company did. |
+| Score and particulars are columns next to the level | Only the level, as the policy team asked | The level alone cannot show that one `company_specific` passage gives one particular and another five. The level stays the field the policy team sees. |
+| `single` stage kept under v0.10 | Remove the one-call path | Without it the two-call design cannot be compared on the same codebook and passages. |
+
+**Better.** Nothing measured yet. What can be said: a complete v0.10 label passes the same
+acceptance check as before; the level can no longer be given without the ticks that justify it;
+the roles codebook can be changed without touching the details codebook.
+
+**Not better / open.**
+
+- Not run on any passage. Model names for the two calls are open (all runs so far used
+  `gemini-2.5-flash`; `cloud/config.env` names `gemini-3.8-flash`, unused).
+- Cost is not known. The first call goes to every passage, the second to all passages about AI
+  (about 90 % on `us-2025h1_V3`), so the saving is in codebook length per call, not in requests;
+  whether a stronger first model makes the run dearer than V3 is to be measured on the subset.
+- A roles error now propagates into a separate call instead of the same answer; the details call
+  cannot correct it. Entry 12 measured that adding fields moves the roles; removing fields from
+  the call may move them too.
+- The ten particulars were written from the definitions of v0.9 and from Hope et al.'s entity
+  classes, not from data; whether coders tick them consistently is untested. The V3 raters scored
+  five levels, so on the subset only the derived three levels are comparable with the human labels
+  (`boilerplate` and `generic` to `generic`, `specific` to `company_specific`, `detailed` and
+  `quantified` to `detailed`; `SUBSTANTIVENESS_V09_TO_V010` in `analysis/human_validation/human_validation.py`).
+  Rater sheets for a v0.10 validation need columns for the particulars; not built.
+- The details codebook still carries the whole MIT table (45,000 characters) and is read by the
+  cheaper model.
+
+**Subset test (2026-10-31).** The 290 passages of the V3 validation sample
+(`gs://airo-pipeline-data/us-2025h1_V3/human_validation/sample.jsonl`), labelled two ways under v0.10
+with `analysis/v010_subset/subset_test.py`; tasks, codebooks, labels, run files and the agreement
+table are in `gs://version10test/`. Two calls: roles with `gemini-3.5-flash` (thinking at the model's
+default, endpoint `global`; the model is listed for `us-central1` but answers 404 there), details with
+`gemini-2.5-flash` (thinking off, `us-central1`, as in V3). One call: `gemini-2.5-flash` with the
+v0.10 single codebook. 40 passages per request in both. Agreement with the seven raters (348 human
+ratings; the raters' five substantiveness levels mapped to three), Cohen's kappa:
+
+| Field | V3 model, v0.9 | two calls | one call |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| ai_reference | 0.75 | 0.86 | 0.76 |
+| frame | 0.68 | 0.72 | 0.69 |
+| roles (exact set) | 0.50 | 0.52 | 0.52 |
+| roles:risk | 0.75 | 0.77 | 0.77 |
+| roles:adoption | 0.55 | 0.54 | 0.57 |
+| roles:governance | 0.55 | 0.52 | 0.53 |
+| roles:market_driver | 0.49 | 0.55 | 0.48 |
+| risk_types (exact set) | 0.52 | 0.56 | 0.54 |
+| mit_subdomains (exact set) | 0.34 | 0.38 | 0.34 |
+| adoption_stage | 0.43 | 0.42 | 0.38 |
+| business_functions (exact set) | 0.30 | 0.32 | 0.31 |
+| governance_responses (exact set) | 0.34 | 0.36 | 0.38 |
+| substantiveness (three levels) | 0.35 | 0.22 | 0.22 |
+| disclosure_traits (exact set) | 0.21 | 0.15 | 0.24 |
+
+Labelled: 290 of 290 (V3), 288 of 290 (each v0.10 variant; two evidence quotes not verbatim).
+Tokens: roles call 143,121 input, 28,024 output, 83,874 thinking for 290 passages; details call
+546,521 input, 54,526 output for 239 passages, of which 31 were asked again on their own with the
+whole details codebook (29 then accepted), so the re-asks are most of its input; one call 308,421
+input, 69,371 output for 290 passages.
+
+What the figures say:
+
+- The stronger roles model moves `ai_reference` (0.75 to 0.86) and `frame` (0.68 to 0.72) and
+  `market_driver`; the roles as a set move from 0.50 to 0.52 in both variants, within what 290
+  passages can resolve. The two calls did not lower any dependent field; `risk_types` and
+  `mit_subdomains` are slightly higher with the roles given.
+- The count-based substantiveness is worse against the raters than the five rated levels mapped to
+  three (0.22 against 0.35), in both variants, so it is the criteria and not the call design. The
+  cross-tabulation (two calls, explicit on both sides): where a rater said `boilerplate` or
+  `generic` (176 ratings), the model gave `company_specific` 141 times and `generic` 17 times; the
+  ticks behind that are `mechanism` (101) and `named_function` (45). Read against the passages,
+  `mechanism` was ticked for generic causal chains ("as we increase the use of AI tools, the risk of
+  unauthorized access may increase") although its criterion asks for a step particular to this
+  company, and `named_function` for "our operations", "our platforms", "our business". The two
+  criteria need the exclusions written out; the eight others were ticked rarely enough that the
+  subset says little about them. The model's distribution (176 `company_specific`, 45 `detailed`,
+  16 `generic`; scores 0 to 4) is the result of these two ticks.
+- Part of the gap is definitional: v0.9's `generic` was "names an area, nothing specific to the
+  company", so a rater's `generic` for "AI in customer service" maps to `generic`, while v0.10
+  ticks `named_function` for the same words. Whether a named function alone should make a passage
+  `company_specific` is a decision, not a model error.
+
+Open after the subset: tighten `mechanism` and `named_function` (or move `named_function` out of
+group S) and re-run the details call only (the roles labels stand); fix the details re-asks, which
+sent the whole codebook 31 times; decide the `global` endpoint for the roles model in
+`cloud/config.env`.
+
+---
+
+## 19. `frame` removed (v0.10)
+
+**Before (v0.4 to v0.9, entry 10).** A required field `frame` (`risk_factor`, `governance`,
+`business`, `market`), fixed from section, heading and sentence function before any role or trait,
+and four criteria that were read in that frame: `adoption` in frame `governance` needed a clause
+outside the remit; `governance` was the primary role in its frame; `market_driver` was the primary
+role in frame `market` and needed an explicit demand statement elsewhere; `externalisation` was never
+set in frame `market`. On `us-2025h1_V3` the raters agreed with each other on `frame` at kappa 0.72
+and with the model at 0.68.
+
+**New** (team decision of 2026-10-31; `src/airo/taxonomy.py`, `classify.py`, `aggregate.py`,
+`export.py`, `coders/gemini.py`). The field is gone from the schema, the codebooks, the adjudication,
+the report summary and the export. The order of coding is `ai_reference`, `roles`, then the
+role-dependent fields (`risk_types`, `mit_subdomains`, ...). What the frame did for the roles is now in
+the role criteria and decision rules: use asserted inside an oversight, review or control remit is
+`governance` and not `adoption`; `market_driver` needs an explicit demand, price, supply or investment
+statement in the passage; `externalisation` applies only with a role that puts the AI in the company's
+own hands or risks (`adoption`, `offering`, `governance`, `risk`, `incident`), which the schema enforces
+in place of the old frame rule. The roles codebook asks the coder to judge each role from the function
+of the sentence the AI statement stands in, with section and heading as context; that is the frame
+decision without a field for it.
+
+**Why.** The frame was an intermediate step whose only consumers were the role criteria. A field that
+is not reported, with its own disagreement (kappa 0.72 among raters), costs rating time and a place
+where a disagreement can start before the roles.
+
+**Better.** Subset re-run under this version on 2026-10-31 (same 290 passages, models and settings as
+in entry 18; the details call now defers its re-asks and runs them as one batch pass: 4 of 242 passages,
+12,726 input tokens instead of the 31 single re-asks of round 1). Agreement with the seven raters,
+Cohen's kappa, next to the V3 model and round 1:
+
+| Field | V3 model, v0.9 | two calls, with frame (round 1) | two calls, no frame | one call, no frame |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| ai_reference | 0.75 | 0.86 | 0.89 | 0.84 |
+| roles (exact set) | 0.50 | 0.52 | 0.56 | 0.56 |
+| roles:risk | 0.75 | 0.77 | 0.76 | 0.75 |
+| roles:adoption | 0.55 | 0.54 | 0.59 | 0.64 |
+| roles:offering | 0.44 | 0.45 | 0.48 | 0.52 |
+| roles:governance | 0.55 | 0.52 | 0.55 | 0.60 |
+| roles:market_driver | 0.49 | 0.55 | 0.65 | 0.49 |
+| risk_types (exact set) | 0.52 | 0.56 | 0.53 | 0.54 |
+| mit_subdomains (exact set) | 0.34 | 0.38 | 0.35 | 0.36 |
+| adoption_stage | 0.43 | 0.42 | 0.47 | 0.47 |
+| business_functions (exact set) | 0.30 | 0.32 | 0.36 | 0.36 |
+| governance_responses (exact set) | 0.34 | 0.36 | 0.44 | 0.43 |
+| substantiveness (three levels) | 0.35 | 0.22 | 0.21 | 0.23 |
+| disclosure_traits (exact set) | 0.21 | 0.15 | 0.13 | 0.21 |
+
+All 290 passages labelled in both frame-free variants. Tokens, no frame: roles call 137,634 input,
+33,882 output, 75,177 thinking (290 passages); details call 171,906 + 12,726 input, 51,309 + 1,109
+output (242 passages asked, 48 labelled without a request); one call 235,040 input, 64,129 output.
+Without the frame the roles as a set move from 0.50 (V3) and 0.52 (round 1) to 0.56 in both variants,
+with `adoption`, `offering`, `governance` and `market_driver` up and `risk` unchanged; the dependent
+fields `business_functions` and `governance_responses` follow the roles up. The one-call variant with
+the cheaper model is level with the two calls on the roles and ahead on `adoption` and `governance`;
+the stronger roles model is ahead on `ai_reference` and `market_driver`. `substantiveness` is
+unchanged at 0.21 to 0.23: the two criteria named in entry 18 are still open. The disclosure traits
+fall to 0.13 in the two-call variant, which the subset cannot explain; the traits were the weakest
+field in every run so far (human-human kappa 0.15). One field fewer for raters and for the roles call.
+
+**Not better / open.** The v0.4 argument for the frame was that it gives a disagreement a location one
+step earlier ("we disagree on the frame" before "we disagree on the role"); that location is gone, and
+a disagreement on `adoption` against `governance` now has to be read from the role criteria alone. The
+human-validation sheets of V3 carry `frame`; the collection code keeps reading it
+(`FRAMES_V09` in `analysis/human_validation/human_validation.py`). Labels of v0.9 and earlier have the
+field, v0.10 labels do not; a comparison across versions drops it.
+
